WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
OK.

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:04.500
Let's get into audience questions for the
week, one that is increasingly coming up in

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.500
different forms.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.870
Is this question of Donald Trump and the federal
government versus states when it comes to

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:15.470
shelter in place, stay at home orders and
shutting down versus reopening the economy.

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.530
And the question is, it was received received
today from Mark was.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:23.460
Hey, David, if Donald Trump wants to reopen
the economy before state governors are willing

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.460
to.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.820
Is there any way Trump can overrule a governor?

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:28.820
Stay at home.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.820
Order.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.190
I did a bunch of research on this and the
answer to me strikes me as no.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:40.739
From all the legal opinions that I've read
about this, so Trump can do what he wants

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:41.739
federally.

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:45.649
He can say we're going to extend the guidelines
to April 30th, as he did last week over the

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:48.020
on his Sunday press conference.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:53.949
And when it comes to federal employees, Trump
has certain authority when it comes to international

00:00:53.949 --> 00:00:55.510
air travel.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.679
It doesn't appear as though, for example,
if if Trump bans travel from China to the

00:01:00.679 --> 00:01:06.729
U.S., it doesn't appear as though Massachusetts
Governor Charlie Baker could say, no, no,

00:01:06.729 --> 00:01:11.479
no, we're going to allow planes from China
to land at Boston's Logan Airport.

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:13.119
Doesn't seem to be the case.

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:18.719
Trump's designation sort of rules or supersedes
whatever a local government governor might

00:01:18.719 --> 00:01:19.719
want to do there.

00:01:19.719 --> 00:01:24.109
Of course, you've got the FAA regulating air
travel as a federal agency, but I think you

00:01:24.109 --> 00:01:26.240
sort of get the point.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.579
Governors do not work for the federal government
nor for Donald Trump.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:32.850
They are administrators of their state.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.149
And we have states rights here.

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:41.140
So if Donald Trump says everybody's got to
get back to work and Charlie Baker here or

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:46.649
Andrew Cuomo or Gavin Newsom or whoever says,
no, we're not going to do that.

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:51.920
It might seem as though there is a very significant
conflict there and it might sort of become

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:52.920
a fight.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:02:00.630
Now, the reality is that these shelter in
place orders are not really being enforced

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.090
significantly by police at this point other
than in some cases where there have been no

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:09.170
big gatherings that police broke up in arrested
people for the most part.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:12.610
And in Massachusetts, we have experience from
the Boston marathon bombing when there was

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.140
a shelter in place, when the bombers were
on the loose.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.170
These are basically things that are followed
by people not at the end of a barrel of a

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.260
gun, so to speak.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:30.700
And in in that same way, I believe that there
is not really any way for the federal government

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.150
to come in and do the opposite and say, you've
got to go to work now.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.200
And I'm pretty confident in Massachusetts.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.330
If Trump said, let's read, let it rip.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:43.530
And Charlie Baker says let's not, that it's
not even going to be an issue that people

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.990
are simply going to do what Charlie Baker
says.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:50.760
Now, the most likely outcome from a legal
perspective, if the Trump administration wanted

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.700
to push it, imagine that federally Trump lifts
the guidelines and governors say we're going

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.210
to keep doing our thing and we're keeping
things close.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.650
The federal government could sue a state that
happens.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:06.560
There are lawsuits between federal and state
governments very regularly.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.270
And the litigation would take a very, very
long time, likely longer than the Corona virus

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.050
shut down would last in any of these states.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:17.540
And in the meantime, states would remain closed.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.060
There's one other element I want to talk about.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:26.720
The 10th Amendment has an antique commandeering
interpretation which prevents the federal

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.610
government from having local police enforce
federal laws.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:38.330
So, for example, when California decriminalized
is or legalizes marijuana, it's illegal federally.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:44.040
The federal government doesn't tell you no
Santa Monica police to go enforce the federal

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.260
illegality of marijuana.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:51.490
They send in the DEA or the ATF or whoever
it is that they send in federal agencies in

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:56.200
the same way National Guards are administered
at the state.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:02.810
Who is it that the federal government would
send in to enforce at the state level that

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.950
the federal government wants things reopened?

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:07.180
Hard to imagine.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:11.770
So on a practical level, I believe governors
would have the upper hand if they decide to

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.070
keep things closed on a legal level.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:15.380
It could be a fight.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.450
It's a fight that legally could take years
and would long outlast the actual likely durations

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:22.790
of these stay at home orders.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.200
Shelter in place.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.610
Nonessential business shut down sort of thing.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:27.840
That's my best estimate.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.360
Now, one other thing to mention.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.510
I read one take online that said now, you
know, the federal government could enforce

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:35.510
that.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.770
Businesses must open under the commerce clause.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:46.010
I found no serious legal analyst that was
willing to to to make that case.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:48.270
And it strikes me as pretty bogus.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:51.640
But if you disagree, if you believe that there
is a way that the federal government could

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:57.200
enforce an open everything order, even if
a governor doesn't want to let me know.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.200
I don't see it.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.200
I haven't found a single.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:00.130
Credible legal expert that sees it that way.

